1. A bag contains 5 red, 4 green, and 3 blue balls. If two balls are drawn at random without replacement, what is the probability that both balls are of different colors?
(Options seen in exams: a) 47/66 b) 35/66 c) 5/11 d) None of these)

ans: Total balls = 12, Total ways = C(12,2) = 66
P(same color) = [C(5,2) + C(4,2) + C(3,2)] / 66 = [10 + 6 + 3] / 66 = 19/66
P(different color) = 1 − 19/66 = 47/66 ✔

2.. A box contains 4 white and 6 black shirts. Two shirts are drawn one by one without replacement. Given that the first shirt drawn is black, what is the probability that the second shirt is also black?
(Options: a) 1/3 b) 5/9 c) 5/3 d) 1/2)

ans: After 1 black shirt is removed → 9 shirts remain (4 white, 5 black)
P(2nd black | 1st was black) = 5/9 ≈ 0.556 ✔


3.A box contains 5 red, 4 blue, and 3 green shirts. One shirt is drawn at random. Find the probability that it is (a) red, (b) not blue, (c) red or green.
5 Red
4 Blue
3 Green
Total: 12
ans: Total shirts = 12
(a) P(red) = 5/12 ≈ 0.417
(b) P(not blue) = (5+3)/12 = 8/12 = 2/3
(c) P(red or green) = (5+3)/12 = 8/12 = 2/3
